    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="band">
        <h1>sqlite_query_forge <span class="t-red">quick start</span></h1>
        <p class="lede">
          A zero-cost declarative query builder in Dart 3: sealed expressions,
          immutable builders, and every runtime value bound as a parameter.
          Reads, writes, transactions, and the bridge back to the schema
          package, with no bundled driver and no code generation.
        </p>
        <div class="meta">
          <span class="ver">0.1.0</span>
          <a href="https://pub.dev/packages/sqlite_query_forge">pub.dev</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/albetnov/the_forge_ecosystem/tree/main/packages/sqlite_query_forge">README source</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ol class="toc" aria-label="Stops on this line">
        <li><a href="#install"><span class="stop">S1</span>Install the line</a></li>
        <li><a href="#design"><span class="stop">S2</span>Three design ideas</a></li>
        <li><a href="#reads"><span class="stop">S3</span>Reads: select, filter, join</a></li>
        <li><a href="#writes"><span class="stop">S4</span>Writes: insert, upsert, return</a></li>
        <li><a href="#tx"><span class="stop">S5</span>Transactions and compiled SQL</a></li>
        <li><a href="#bridge"><span class="stop">S6</span>Bridge back to schema</a></li>
        <li><a href="#skills"><span class="stop">S7</span>Agent skills</a></li>
      </ol>

      <div class="prose">
        <section class="block" id="install" aria-labelledby="install-h">
          <h2 id="install-h">Install the line</h2>
          <p>
            One dependency for the builder. It reaches the database through a
            <code class="inline">ForgeExecutor</code> you implement over the binding you already use, so plan on one small adapter (stop S6). It also depends on <code class="inline">sqlite_schema_forge</code> for the migration bridge, and ships no driver of its own.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>terminal</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-install">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-install" set:html={highlight(installCmd)} /></pre>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="design" aria-labelledby="design-h">
          <h2 id="design-h">Three design ideas</h2>
          <p>Everything in the query builder follows from three choices:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Sealed types.</strong> <code class="inline">Expression</code> is a sealed hierarchy, so a switch over it is exhaustively checked, and adding a node fails to compile until it is handled.</li>
            <li><strong>Immutability and const.</strong> A <code class="inline">SelectBuilder</code> is immutable, so any chain can be shared, cached, or built conditionally. <code class="inline">Table</code> and <code class="inline">Column</code> declarations are <code class="inline">const</code> and canonicalized, so a column reference costs no allocation the second time.</li>
            <li><strong>Values are always bound.</strong> Every runtime value becomes a <code class="inline">?</code> parameter. SQL text is safe by construction, and prepared statements cache and reuse across argument values. Identifiers are quoted in exactly one place.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="note">
            <p><span class="lead">Hot path:</span> a filter such as <code class="inline">users.id.eq(42)</code> allocates exactly one node. The column on its left is an existing <code class="inline">const</code> object, not a fresh wrapper.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="reads" aria-labelledby="reads-h">
          <h2 id="reads-h">Reads: select, filter, join</h2>
          <p>
            Declare the table once as a const object with an explicit constructor, then query through the <code class="inline">Forge</code> facade. The table mapper runs for you; project into records when a full model is more than you need.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>the table</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-table">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-table" set:html={highlight(tableSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>select with mapper</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-select">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-select" set:html={highlight(selectSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <h3>Filters compose</h3>
          <p>
            Builders are immutable, so a chain builds up conditionally with no tracking of which filter came first:
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>conditional chain</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-filter">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-filter" set:html={highlight(filterSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <h3>Joins stay explicit</h3>
          <p>
            Column-to-column equality gets its own spelling (<code class="inline">sameAs</code>), since Dart cannot overload on argument type. Qualify whichever side needs it and match the alias the join binds:
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>left join with alias</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-join">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-join" set:html={highlight(joinSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="writes" aria-labelledby="writes-h">
          <h2 id="writes-h">Writes: insert, upsert, return</h2>
          <p>
            Writes start from the same facade and stay immutable. An <code class="inline">UPDATE</code> or <code class="inline">DELETE</code> with no <code class="inline">where</code> touches every row, so read that twice.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>insert, update, delete</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-write">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-write" set:html={highlight(writeSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <h3>Upserts reach excluded</h3>
          <p>
            A value that is an expression is written as a reference rather than bound, which is how the <code class="inline">excluded</code> row is reached:
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>ignore or update on conflict</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-upsert">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-upsert" set:html={highlight(upsertSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <h3>Returning turns writes into reads</h3>
          <p>Rows come back through <code class="inline">getMany()</code> instead of a rowid or a count. Needs SQLite 3.35 or newer.</p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>read back what you wrote</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-returning">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-returning" set:html={highlight(returningSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="tx" aria-labelledby="tx-h">
          <h2 id="tx-h">Transactions and compiled SQL</h2>
          <p>
            <code class="inline">transaction</code> hands the action a <code class="inline">Forge</code> bound to the transaction, and every statement issued through it joins that transaction. A nested call joins the open one rather than issuing a second <code class="inline">BEGIN</code>; a throw rolls back and propagates.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>nested joins the open transaction</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-tx">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-tx" set:html={highlight(txSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <p>
            Every builder exposes the same compile step twice: <code class="inline">compile()</code> for SQL plus arguments together, <code class="inline">toSql()</code> and <code class="inline">getRawArgs()</code> for one or the other. Log it, test against it, paste it into a SQLite shell:
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>read the SQL it emits</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-compile">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-compile" set:html={highlight(compileSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="bridge" aria-labelledby="bridge-h">
          <h2 id="bridge-h">Bridge back to schema</h2>
          <p>
            Columns can also declare how they are created, so the query path and the migration path read one declaration: the named constructors (<code class="inline">Column.id</code>, <code class="inline">.integer</code>, <code class="inline">.text</code>) attach DDL flags to the very column the queries use. <code class="inline">schemaOf</code> collects tables under one version, and <code class="inline">ForgeDatabaseExecutor</code> presents a <code class="inline">ForgeExecutor</code> as the migrator's <code class="inline">DatabaseExecutor</code>, so one adapter drives both halves.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>migrate what you query</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-bridge">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-bridge" set:html={highlight(bridgeSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <p>The adapter itself is one small class over the binding in use:</p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>one adapter, both halves</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-adapter">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-adapter" set:html={highlight(adapterSnippet)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <div class="note">
            <p><span class="lead">No code generation:</span> declarations stay hand-written. Indexes, virtual tables, triggers, and composite keys stay in the migration schema, where those concepts already live.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="block" id="skills" aria-labelledby="skills-h">
          <h2 id="skills-h">Agent skills</h2>
          <p>
            <code class="inline">sqlite_query_forge</code> also ships its
            guidance as agent skills: prescriptive rules and examples authored
            for Cursor, Claude Code, Copilot, Cline, and any other agent working
            in your repository. Each skill is a <code class="inline">SKILL.md</code>
            shipped inside the package, so the advice is versioned with the API
            you actually installed.
          </p>
          <figure class="code">
            <figcaption><span>install the package skills</span><button class="copy" data-copy="code-skills">Copy</button></figcaption>
            <pre><code id="code-skills" set:html={highlight(skillsCmd)} /></pre>
          </figure>
          <p>Five skills ship with <code class="inline">sqlite_query_forge</code>:</p>
          <table class="scope">
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">sqlite-query-forge-forge</th><td>The Forge entrypoint and the ForgeExecutor contract: queries, writes, raw statements, transactions.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">sqlite-query-forge-select</th><td>Typed SELECTs: the SelectBuilder chain and the getMany, getSingle, getFirst, count terminals.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">sqlite-query-forge-writes</th><td>INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE builders: values, returning, onConflict, execute vs getMany.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">sqlite-query-forge-expressions</th><td>Typed expressions: qualify, RawSql, SqlFragment, bound args, AND/OR precedence.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">sqlite-query-forge-schema-bridge</th><td>One declaration, both halves: Table.columns to a schema table, and ForgeDatabaseExecutor adapters.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <div class="note">
            <p><span class="lead">Where they land:</span> run the command in an app that already depends on <code class="inline">sqlite_query_forge</code>. The installer checks each directory's package prefix and writes the matching skills to your project's <code class="inline">.agents/skills/</code>.</p>
          </div>
        </section>
